#
# Per-rig sound init overrides -- copy to local/sound.tcl ONLY if this rig
# needs a different init SEQUENCE. Creating this file REPLACES the default
# init entirely: a file that exists and never calls an init leaves the rig
# silent with nothing saying so.
#
# You do NOT need this file for the common rig facts -- those are declared
# (settings gear -> local/rig.tcl):
#
#   setting sound device       auto|default|plughw:CARD=Device,DEV=0
#   setting sound output_class auto|usb|i2s|analog|hdmi|none
#   setting sound shared       0|1    (dmix, so dserv AND stim2 can play)
#   setting sound soundfont    default|<name.sf2>|/abs/path.sf2
#
# A declaration wins over anything this file sets (soundconf consults the
# declarations at use time), and legacy variables set here (output_shared,
# output_class) are adopted into rig.tcl on first boot. With no file at
# all, the standard init runs with the declared values.
#
# If you DO need this file, end it with the standard init calls -- they
# pull the declared values themselves:
#
# sound::init_hardware        ;# hardware MIDI on a serial port, if present
# sound::init_software        ;# fluidsynth on the resolved output
#
# Exotic examples of why this file might exist:
#
# Pin one of two identical USB dongles by usb id glob:
# sound::init_software default auto "USB_Audio_Device*"
#
# Hand-built fluidsynth init -- exact font + exact device, no resolution:
# sound::init_fluidsynth /usr/share/sounds/sf2/default-GM.sf2 plughw:CARD=Device,DEV=0
#
# Hardware MIDI on a nonstandard serial port:
# soundOpen /dev/ttyUSB1
# soundReset
#
# Troubleshooting: if the card is held by another process, try
#   sudo fuser -v /dev/snd/*
# and on desktop boxes consider stopping pipewire:
#   systemctl --user stop pipewire pipewire-pulse wireplumber
